            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "As mentioned in Table B.15 and Table B.16, it is optional to include the \"certificatePolicies\" extension in any of the cross certificates. If the \"certificatePolicies\" extension is included, it can be marked as non-critical so that the relying party can ignore this extension if it cannot process it. This is done to improve the interoperability.",
            "block_bbox": [
                67,
                1138,
                1054,
                1244
            ],
            "block_id": 3,
            "block_order": 1,
            "group_id": 3,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    67.0,
                    1138.0
                ],
                [
                    1054.0,
                    1138.0
                ],
                [
                    1054.0,
                    1244.0
                ],
                [
                    67.0,
                    1244.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "paragraph_title",
            "block_content": "[V2G20-2601]",
            "block_bbox": [
                70,
                1265,
                219,
                1292
            ],
            "block_id": 4,
            "block_order": 2,
            "group_id": 4,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    70.0,
                    1265.0
                ],
                [
                    219.0,
                    1265.0
                ],
                [
                    219.0,
                    1292.0
                ],
                [
                    70.0,
                    1292.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "Cross certificate shall contain at least one or both of CRLDistributionPoints or/and AuthorityInfoAccess extensions.",
            "block_bbox": [
                252,
                1265,
                1053,
                1317
            ],
            "block_id": 5,
            "block_order": 3,
            "group_id": 5,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    252.0,
                    1265.0
                ],
                [
                    1053.0,
                    1265.0
                ],
                [
                    1053.0,
                    1317.0
                ],
                [
                    252.0,
                    1317.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "NOTE 1 Table B.15 and Table B.16 specify that both CRLDistributionPoints and AuthorityInfoAccess extensions are optional but critical to indicate that one of them is included and processed. [V2G20-2601] clarifies that including at least one of them is mandatory while including both of them is not mandatory.",
            "block_bbox": [
                67,
                1341,
                1053,
                1413
            ],
            "block_id": 6,
            "block_order": 4,
            "group_id": 6,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    67.0,
                    1341.0
                ],
                [
                    1053.0,
                    1341.0
                ],
                [
                    1053.0,
                    1413.0
                ],
                [
                    67.0,
                    1413.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "NOTE 2 [V2G20-2601] does not specify which one of these extensions can be included. It is left up to the CA to decide which certificate revocation method works best for it. In some cases CRLs will work better as they allow the relying party to download the entire CRL once and have it available for local checking of revocation status of the next contract certificate without a need for a live connection to the CRL server while in other cases OCSP will provide better results as it provides current revocation status and requires less storage and searching on part of the relying party.",
            "block_bbox": [
                68,
                1435,
                1054,
                1579
            ],
